# The rvabi sample directory tracks its OUTPUT and ignores its INPUTS.
#
# The distinction is the whole point of the course: a number in the prose is
# a claim about BYTES, so the bytes' derived report is committed and the
# things that make the bytes are not.  A reader can check every figure in the
# course against rvabi.out, run1.txt and run2.txt WITHOUT installing a
# cross-compiler -- which on this host means without clang, without a riscv64
# target and without a RISC-V linker, none of which are needed because nothing
# in this course is ever executed.
#
# A reader who wants to re-derive them runs build_samples.sh and needs clang
# with a riscv64 and an aarch64 target plus llvm-objdump-21.  There is still
# no RISC-V BINUTILS on this host, so there is no second, independent
# assembler: the two readers ultimately depend on ONE LLVM TREE, and the
# artifact says so in section 1 rather than implying an independence it does
# not have.
#
# So: the recorded runs are tracked, the objects are not.  They are large,
# they are binary, and they are reproducible from four text files with one
# command.

# Object files.  All of these are outputs of build_samples.sh -- the four C
# corpora at four levels, the same three at four -march settings, the
# aarch64 and x86-64 builds of the same C, and the hand-written probe.  The
# `*.o` pattern is listed once as a pattern and every name is NOT listed
# again, because a name that appears in two places is a name that eventually
# disagrees with itself.
*.o

# The disassembler's own listing, for the same reason: it is derived, and it
# is the SECOND reader's opinion of a file this course also reads by hand.
*_objdump.txt

# Python bytecode.  Present on disk because rvabi.py imports the sibling
# course's rvdec.py, and the import writes this directory's cache; it is never
# an input to anything.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
